<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="chapter12_portable-binary-formats-endianness-and-serialization">
    <title>Portable Binary Formats, Endianness, and Serialization</title>

    <p>
        A binary representation that works on one machine is not automatically a
        portable file format. A portable format should document field meanings,
        field widths, byte order, padding rules, and version information.
    </p>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter12_endianness">
        <title>Endianness</title>
        <p>
            <term>Endianness</term> describes the order in which the bytes of a
            multi-byte value are stored. Consider the 32-bit hexadecimal value
            <c>0x01020304</c>:
        </p>

        <table xml:id="table_chapter12_endianness">
            <title>Two common byte orders</title>
            <tabular top="major" left="major" bottom="major" right="major">
                <col width="22%"/><col width="28%"/><col width="50%"/>
                <row header="yes">
                    <cell>Byte order</cell>
                    <cell>Lowest-address byte</cell>
                    <cell>Byte sequence</cell>
                </row>
                <row><cell>Big-endian</cell><cell>Most significant byte</cell><cell><c>01 02 03 04</c></cell></row>
                <row><cell>Little-endian</cell><cell>Least significant byte</cell><cell><c>04 03 02 01</c></cell></row>
            </tabular>
        </table>

        <p>
            Endianness does not change the mathematical value when a processor
            interprets its own representation. It matters when raw bytes cross a
            machine, file-format, or network boundary. C++17 has no standard
            <c>std::endian</c> facility; that facility was added in C++20.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter12_fixed-width-portable-values">
        <title>Fixed-width values do not choose byte order</title>
        <p>
            A type such as <c>std::uint32_t</c> communicates that the value uses
            exactly 32 bits when the implementation provides that type. It does
            not say whether the most significant or least significant byte comes
            first. A binary format must specify both the width and the byte order.
        </p>

        <program xml:id="program_chapter12_explicit-byte-order"
                 language="cpp" line-numbers="yes">
            <code><![CDATA[
#include <cstdint>

void write_big_endian(std::uint32_t value, unsigned char bytes[4]) {
    bytes[0] = static_cast<unsigned char>(value >> 24);
    bytes[1] = static_cast<unsigned char>(value >> 16);
    bytes[2] = static_cast<unsigned char>(value >> 8);
    bytes[3] = static_cast<unsigned char>(value);
}
            ]]></code>
        </program>
        <p>
            This function constructs a documented big-endian representation
            rather than copying the machine's native object bytes. A reader can
            reverse the process by shifting the bytes back into a 32-bit value.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter12_serialization">
        <title>Safe serialization</title>
        <p>
            <term>Serialization</term> converts meaningful program data into a
            storable or transferable representation. <term>Deserialization</term>
            reconstructs program data from that representation. A safe design
            serializes fields explicitly rather than blindly writing an entire
            object.
        </p>

        <program xml:id="program_chapter12_explicit-serialization"
                 language="cpp" line-numbers="yes">
            <code><![CDATA[
#include <cstdint>
#include <ostream>

struct ScoreRecord {
    std::uint32_t student_id;
    std::int32_t score;
};

void write_score_record(
    std::ostream& output,
    const ScoreRecord& record
) {
    output.write(
        reinterpret_cast<const char*>(&record.student_id),
        sizeof record.student_id
    );
    output.write(
        reinterpret_cast<const char*>(&record.score),
        sizeof record.score
    );
}
            ]]></code>
        </program>
        <p>
            This example identifies the fields in the format, but it still uses
            native byte order. A fully portable version would encode each fixed-
            width value according to the format's documented byte order.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter12_binary-format-versioning">
        <title>Version and validation information</title>
        <p>
            A durable binary format can begin with a magic value and a version
            number. A reader can reject an unknown version before interpreting
            the remaining bytes. It should also check file size, record counts,
            field ranges, and read success rather than trusting arbitrary input.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter12_class-persistence">
        <title>Persisting a class object</title>

        <p>
            A class object can be persisted by saving the meaningful values of
            its data members and later using those values to reconstruct another
            object. The program should not copy the class object's entire memory
            representation, especially when the class owns strings, pointers, or
            other resources.
        </p>

        <listing xml:id="listing_chapter12_class-persistence">
            <title>Saving and loading a class object</title>
            <program xml:id="program_chapter12_class-persistence"
                     language="cpp" line-numbers="yes">
                <code><![CDATA[
#include <cstdint>
#include <fstream>
#include <iostream>
#include <istream>
#include <ostream>

class ScoreRecord {
private:
    std::uint32_t student_id{};
    std::int32_t score{};

public:
    ScoreRecord() = default;

    ScoreRecord(std::uint32_t starting_id, std::int32_t starting_score)
        : student_id{starting_id}, score{starting_score} {
    }

    void save_to(std::ostream& output) const {
        output.write(
            reinterpret_cast<const char*>(&student_id),
            sizeof student_id
        );
        output.write(
            reinterpret_cast<const char*>(&score),
            sizeof score
        );
    }

    bool load_from(std::istream& input) {
        std::uint32_t loaded_id{};
        std::int32_t loaded_score{};

        if (!input.read(
            reinterpret_cast<char*>(&loaded_id),
            sizeof loaded_id
        )) {
            return false;
        }

        if (!input.read(
            reinterpret_cast<char*>(&loaded_score),
            sizeof loaded_score
        )) {
            return false;
        }

        student_id = loaded_id;
        score = loaded_score;
        return true;
    }

    void print_summary() const {
        std::cout << "Student " << student_id
                  << ": " << score << '\n';
    }
};

int main() {
    ScoreRecord original{1042, 92};

    {
        std::ofstream output_file{
            "score-record.bin",
            std::ios::binary
        };

        if (!output_file) {
            return 1;
        }

        original.save_to(output_file);
    }

    ScoreRecord restored{};
    {
        std::ifstream input_file{
            "score-record.bin",
            std::ios::binary
        };

        if (!input_file || !restored.load_from(input_file)) {
            return 1;
        }
    }

    restored.print_summary();
    return 0;
}
                ]]></code>
            </program>
        </listing>

        <p>
            The first object is created in memory, its meaningful fields are
            written to <c>score-record.bin</c>, and a second object is then
            populated by reading those fields. The class definition exists in
            the program both when saving and when loading; the file stores data,
            not executable class definitions.
        </p>

        <p>
            This example uses the machine's native byte order for simplicity.
            A portable version would encode the fixed-width values using the
            file format's specified byte order, add a format version, and check
            limits before allocating or reading data.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <exercise label="chapter12_7_endian-meaning">
        <statement><p>What does endianness describe?</p></statement>
        <choices randomize="yes">
            <choice><statement><p>The order of source files during compilation</p></statement><feedback><p>Compilation order is unrelated to byte order.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice correct="yes"><statement><p>The order of bytes used to store a multi-byte value</p></statement><feedback><p>Big-endian and little-endian systems arrange those bytes differently.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>The number of bits in every integer type</p></statement><feedback><p>Width and byte order are separate properties.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>The order in which files are opened</p></statement><feedback><p>Opening order does not describe memory representation.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>The order of characters in a string only</p></statement><feedback><p>Endianness concerns byte order in multi-byte representations.</p></feedback></choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="chapter12_7_portable-format">
        <statement><p>Select all details a portable binary format should document.</p></statement>
        <choices randomize="yes" multiple-correct="yes">
            <choice correct="yes"><statement><p>The width and meaning of each numeric field</p></statement><feedback><p>The reader needs to know how to interpret each field.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice correct="yes"><statement><p>The byte order for multi-byte values</p></statement><feedback><p>Readers must know how bytes are arranged.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice correct="yes"><statement><p>A version or format identifier</p></statement><feedback><p>Versioning helps readers detect format changes.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>That every pointer address can be saved and reused later</p></statement><feedback><p>Addresses are not portable persisted references.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>That readers may guess unknown fields</p></statement><feedback><p>Explicit rules are safer than guessing.</p></feedback></choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="chapter12_7_endian-code" number="yes" adaptive="yes" indentation="show">
        <statement><p>Arrange the blocks to construct the bytes of a 32-bit value in big-endian order.</p></statement>
        <blocks>
            <block order="1"><cline>std::uint32_t value{0x01020304};</cline></block>
            <block order="2"><cline>bytes[0] = static_cast&lt;unsigned char&gt;(value &gt;&gt; 24);</cline></block>
            <block order="3"><cline>bytes[1] = static_cast&lt;unsigned char&gt;(value &gt;&gt; 16);</cline></block>
            <block order="4"><cline>bytes[2] = static_cast&lt;unsigned char&gt;(value &gt;&gt; 8);</cline></block>
            <block order="5"><cline>bytes[3] = static_cast&lt;unsigned char&gt;(value);</cline></block>
        </blocks>
    </exercise>
</section>
